---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Calculator from '../components/Calculator.astro';
---

<Base title="CPP 2027 rate change — Takehome">
  <Calculator
    code="ON"
    name="Ontario"
    supported
    heading="CPP 2027 rate change"
    lede="This is a PREVIEW of a proposed rate, not an enacted T4127 edition. The number below is not a filing figure."
    defaultAsOf="2027-01-15"
    defaultPayPeriod={52}
    defaultGross="1000.00"
    showProvinceSelect
    resultMode="cpp"
  >
    <div slot="above-number">
      <p>
        <strong>Preview — proposed, not yet enacted.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Announced 2026-04-28 in the Spring Economic Update 2026. There is no
        2027 T4127 edition and no published 2027 YMPE. Do not file from this
        number.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose">
      <h2>What was announced</h2>
      <p>
        On 28 April 2026 the Department of Finance announced that the Canada
        Pension Plan base contribution rate falls from 4.95% to 4.75% on 1
        January 2027. The employee total rate on earnings up to YMPE becomes
        5.75% (4.75% base + 1.00% first additional). CPP2 on YMPE-to-YAMPE
        stays 4%. Employer contributions remain a dollar-for-dollar match.
      </p>
      <p>
        Source:
        <a
          href="https://www.canada.ca/en/department-finance/news/2026/04/spring-economic-update-2026-key-measures.html"
          >Spring Economic Update 2026</a
        >. Bill C-30 received Royal Assent, but this engine still labels the
        2027 rule set <code>proposed</code> until CRA publishes the 2027 T4127
        edition and OSFI/CRA publish the 2027 YMPE. A statute is not a payroll
        table.
      </p>

      <h2>The preview table (2026 YMPE held)</h2>
      <p>
        YMPE $74,600 and YAMPE $85,000 are the 2026 published ceilings. Using
        them here is an explicit holding pattern, not a forecast. Maximums are
        the announced rates applied to those held dollars:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symbol</th>
            <th>2026 (enacted)</th>
            <th>2027 (proposed preview)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>YMPE</td>
            <td>$74,600.00</td>
            <td>$74,600.00 (held; 2027 unpublished)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>YAMPE</td>
            <td>$85,000.00</td>
            <td>$85,000.00 (held; 2027 unpublished)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Basic exemption</td>
            <td>$3,500.00</td>
            <td>$3,500.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Employee / employer rate to YMPE</td>
            <td>5.95% (4.95% + 1.00%)</td>
            <td>5.75% (4.75% + 1.00%)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CPP2 rate YMPE to YAMPE</td>
            <td>4.00%</td>
            <td>4.00%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Max C (employee)</td>
            <td>$4,230.45</td>
            <td>$4,088.25 (base $3,377.25 + first additional $711.00)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Max C2 (employee)</td>
            <td>$416.00</td>
            <td>$416.00</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The calculator above loads rule set <code>2027-01-01</code> and every
        F5 / K2 ratio is <code>base/total</code> from that file — not a
        hard-coded 0.0495/0.0595. Ontario weekly $1,000 is C = $53.63 under the
        preview (it is $55.50 under 2026).
      </p>

      <h2>British Columbia indexation is paused</h2>
      <p>
        BC Budget 2026 pauses personal-income-tax bracket and credit indexation
        for 2027–2030. The preview therefore uses the July 2026 Option 2
        full-year table (lowest rate 5.60%, tax reduction $690) rather than the
        Option 1 mid-year prorated 6.14%. See
        <a href="https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/taxes/tax-updates/budget-changes"
          >BC tax updates</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>EI is a separate announcement</h2>
      <p>
        Maximum insurable earnings and the 2027 EI rate are set under the EI
        Act, not this CPP cut. The preview still uses the 2026 EI table (1.63%
        to $68,900). This page is not an EI-2027 forecast.
      </p>

      <h2>Quebec</h2>
      <p>
        QPP is not CPP. This engine still refuses Quebec rather than print C as
        if the employee were in Ontario. See
        <a href="/calculators/qc/">Quebec</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>When this page will lose the preview label</h2>
      <p>
        The moment CRA publishes the 2027 T4127 edition and the 2027 YMPE is a
        published CRA/OSFI number, rule set <code>2027-01-01</code> becomes
        <code>enacted</code> and the banner above the number comes down. The
        RSS feed is <a href="/changes.xml">/changes.xml</a>. There will not be a
        “projected YMPE” row here in the meantime.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Calculator>
</Base>
